# Title IV — Committee on Commerce, Science, and Transportation

**Sec. 40001. Coast Guard mission readiness.**
Appropriates $24,593,500,000 to the Coast Guard for FY2025, available through September 30, 2029, for new assets, maintenance, shore infrastructure, and operational resilience. Major line items include $4.3 billion each for Offshore Patrol Cutters and Polar Security Cutters, $3.5 billion for Arctic Security Cutters, $2.283 billion for rotary-wing aircraft, $1.1425 billion for fixed-wing aircraft, $4.379 billion for shore facilities (including $425 million for enlisted boot camp barracks, $500 million for the Coast Guard Yard, and $300 million for homeporting the polar icebreaker commissioned in 2025), and $2.2 billion for depot maintenance. Smaller amounts fund Fast Response Cutters ($1 billion), light/medium icebreaking cutters ($816 million), Waterways Commerce Cutters ($162 million), long-range unmanned aircraft ($266 million), maritime domain awareness ($170 million), and autonomous maritime systems ($75 million).

**Sec. 40002. Spectrum auctions.**
Extends the FCC's competitive bidding (auction) authority under section 309(j)(11) of the Communications Act of 1934 to September 30, 2034, while excluding the 3.1–3.45 GHz and 7.4–8.4 GHz bands from auction, reallocation, or withdrawal. The FCC must auction at least 300 megahertz, including completing an auction of at least 100 megahertz in the 3.98–4.2 GHz band within 2 years of enactment. The Assistant Secretary of Commerce (NTIA) must identify an additional 500 megahertz of federally allocated spectrum in the 1.3–10.5 GHz "covered band" for reallocation (at least 200 MHz within 2 years, the rest within 4 years), with auctions of at least 200 MHz completed within 4 years and the remainder within 8 years. The President may modify or withdraw frequencies for national security up to 60 days before an auction, and $50 million is appropriated to the Department of Commerce for spectrum analysis of the 2.7–2.9 GHz, 4.4–4.9 GHz, and 7.25–7.4 GHz bands plus biennial reports on federal spectrum value through June 30, 2034.

**Sec. 40003. Air traffic control improvements.**
Appropriates roughly $12.4 billion to the FAA Administrator for FY2025, available through September 30, 2029, for aviation safety facilities and equipment. The largest items are $4.75 billion for telecommunications infrastructure modernization, $3 billion for radar replacement, $1.9 billion to construct a new air route traffic control center (ARTCC) requiring divestiture of at least 3 existing ARTCCs, and $1 billion for terminal radar approach control (TRACON) recapitalization and consolidation. Other funding includes $500 million for runway safety and surface surveillance, $300 million each for Enterprise Information Display Systems and section 619 of the FAA Reauthorization Act of 2024, $100 million each for closing/consolidating at least 10 ARTCCs and controller advanced training technologies, $80 million for at least 50 Automated Weather Observing Systems, 60 Visual Weather Observing Systems, and 64 weather camera sites, and smaller amounts for remote towers, unstaffed infrastructure, and other FAA Reauthorization Act sections. The FAA Administrator must report expenditures to Congress within 180 days of enactment and every 90 days thereafter.

**Sec. 40004. Space launch and reentry licensing and permitting user fees.**
Directs the Secretary of Transportation to impose per-launch/reentry fees starting in 2026, set at the lesser of a per-pound payload charge (rising from $0.25 in 2026 to $1.50 in 2033, then CPI-indexed) or a flat per-launch cap (rising from $30,000 in 2026 to $200,000 in 2033, then CPI-indexed). Fees are deposited into a new "Office of Commercial Space Transportation Launch and Reentry Licensing and Permitting Fund" in the Treasury, with 70 percent available without further appropriation or fiscal year limitation to fund the FAA's Office of Commercial Space Transportation and section 630(b) of the FAA Reauthorization Act of 2024.

**Sec. 40005. Mars missions, Artemis missions, and Moon to Mars program.**
Appropriates $9,995,000,000 to NASA for FY2025, available through September 30, 2032, with allocations including $2.6 billion for the Gateway program, $4.1 billion for the Space Launch System for Artemis IV and V, $1.25 billion for International Space Station operations, $700 million for a commercially provided Mars telecommunications orbiter (delivered by December 31, 2028), $1 billion for infrastructure at manned spaceflight centers (including $300 million for Johnson Space Center, $250 million for Kennedy Space Center, $120 million for Stennis, $100 million for Marshall, and $30 million for Michoud), $325 million for contract 80JSC024CA002, and $20 million for the Orion vehicle. At least 50 percent of funds must be obligated by September 30, 2028, and 100 percent by September 30, 2029, with all outlays by September 30, 2034. The section also requires the NASA Administrator, within 30 days of enactment, to identify a flown, astronaut-carrying space vehicle for transfer within 18 months to a field center involved in the Commercial Crew Program for public exhibition at an entity in that center's metropolitan area.

**Sec. 40006. Corporate average fuel economy civil penalties.**
Amends 49 U.S.C. § 32912 to reduce the CAFE civil penalty rates from $5 to $0.00 (subsection (b)) and from $10 to $0.00 (subsection (c)(1)(B)), effectively eliminating fines on automakers whose fleet fuel economy falls below applicable standards. The change takes effect on enactment and applies to all model years for which the Secretary of Transportation has not yet issued a penalty notification under section 32903(b)(2)(B).

**Sec. 40007. Payments for lease of Metropolitan Washington Airports.**
Amends 49 U.S.C. § 49104(b) so that the Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority pays the Treasury's general fund $3 million (in 1987 dollars, GNP Price Deflator-adjusted) annually from 1987 through 2026, then $15 million (in 2027 dollars) annually for 2027 and subsequent years. The Secretary of Transportation and the Airports Authority must renegotiate the payment level at least every 10 years, ensuring the annual amount never falls below $15 million in 2027 dollars.

**Sec. 40008. Rescission of certain amounts for the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration.**
Rescinds all unobligated balances appropriated by sections 40001, 40002, 40003, and 40004 of Public Law 117-169 (the CHIPS and Science Act of 2022) to NOAA.

**Sec. 40009. Reduction in annual transfers to Travel Promotion Fund.**
Amends the Travel Promotion Act of 2009 (22 U.S.C. 2131(d)(2)(B)) to cut the annual transfer to the Travel Promotion Fund from $100,000,000 to $20,000,000.

**Sec. 40010. Treatment of unobligated funds for alternative fuel and low-emission aviation technology.**
Rescinds any unobligated balances of the amounts made available by section 40007(a) of title IV of Public Law 117-169 (the CHIPS and Science Act) for alternative fuel and low-emission aviation technology programs (49 U.S.C. 44504 note).

**Sec. 40011. Rescission of amounts appropriated to Public Wireless Supply Chain Innovation Fund.**
Permanently rescinds $850,000,000 of the unobligated balances made available under section 106(a) of the CHIPS Act of 2022 (Public Law 117-167) for the Public Wireless Supply Chain Innovation Fund. [end of text]


